Git and GitHub Commands – Complete Guide
Git is a Version Control System (VCS) used to track changes in source code. GitHub is a cloud-based platform used to store Git repositories and collaborate with developers.
- Git: Manages code versions locally.
- GitHub: Hosts repositories online and supports collaboration.
1. Basic Git Commands
Command	Purpose
git --version	Check Git version
git config --global user.name "Ganesh C"	Set username
git config --global user.email "email"	Set email
git config --list	View Git configuration
git help	Open Git help

2. Repository Commands
Command	Purpose
git init	Initialize a Git repository
git clone URL	Clone a GitHub repository
git status	Check file status
git log	View commit history
git log --oneline	Compact commit history
git log --all --graph --oneline	Visualize branches and commits
git show	Display latest commit details

3. Important Daily Git Commands
Most important for interviews and projects
Command	Purpose
git add filename	Stage a specific file
git add .	Stage all changes in current directory
git commit -m "message"	Save changes as a commit
git push	Upload commits to remote
git pull	Fetch and integrate remote changes
git fetch	Download remote changes without merging
git diff	Show unstaged changes
git diff --staged	Show staged changes

Typical workflow:
git status
git add .
git commit -m "Added student CRUD operations"
git push


4. Git Branch Commands
Branches allow multiple developers to work on different features without affecting the main code.
Command	Purpose
git branch	List local branches
git branch feature-login	Create a branch
git switch feature-login	Switch branch
git switch -c feature-login	Create and switch branch
git checkout branch-name	Switch branch (older syntax)
git merge feature-login	Merge branch into current branch
git branch -d feature-login	Delete merged branch
git branch -D feature-login	Force-delete a branch

Example:
git switch -c feature-authentication

# Make code changes

git add .
git commit -m "Implemented authentication"

git switch main
git merge feature-authentication


5. GitHub Remote Commands
Remote means a repository hosted on GitHub or another server.
Command	Purpose
git remote -v	Display remote URLs
git remote add origin URL	Connect local repository to GitHub
git remote remove origin	Remove remote
git remote set-url origin URL	Change remote URL
git push -u origin main	Push and set upstream
git push origin main	Push main branch
git pull origin main	Pull main branch
git fetch origin	Fetch from GitHub

6. Undo and Reset Commands
Command	Purpose
git restore filename	Discard unstaged file changes
git restore --staged filename	Unstage a file
git reset --soft HEAD~1	Undo last commit, keep staged changes
git reset --mixed HEAD~1	Undo commit, keep working changes unstaged
git reset --hard HEAD~1	Remove last commit and its changes
git revert commit-id	Create a commit that reverses another commit
git stash	Temporarily save changes
git stash pop	Restore stashed changes

Be careful
 git reset --hard can permanently discard uncommitted work.
7. Git Stash Commands
Suppose you are developing a login feature but suddenly need to fix a bug in another branch.
git stash
git switch main

# Fix the bug

git switch feature-login
git stash pop


Other useful commands:
git stash list
git stash apply
git stash drop
git stash clear


8. Git Merge and Conflict Commands
A merge conflict occurs when Git cannot automatically combine changes, commonly because two developers modify the same lines.
git switch main
git merge feature-login


If a conflict occurs:
1. Open the conflicting file.
2. Identify the conflicting code.
3. Choose or combine the correct changes.
4. Remove conflict markers.
5. Stage and commit.
git add .
git commit -m "Resolved merge conflict"


9. Git Tag Commands
Tags are used to mark important versions of a project.
git tag
git tag v1.0
git tag -a v1.0 -m "First release"
git push origin v1.0
git push origin --tags
git tag -d v1.0


10. GitHub Collaboration Commands
Command	Purpose
git clone URL	Download a repository
git fork	No standard Git command; fork using GitHub UI
git remote add upstream URL	Connect original repository
git fetch upstream	Fetch original repository updates
git merge upstream/main	Merge original repository updates
git push origin feature-branch	Push your feature branch


11. Complete Real-Time Project Workflow
Imagine you are working on your Spring Boot Student Management project with two developers.
Developer workflow
GitHub Repository

Central remote repository


Developer 1

Student Service

feature-student


Developer 2

Authentication

feature-auth




Pull Request → Code Review → Merge

Changes integrated into main


Developer 1 commands:
git clone https://github.com/username/project.git

cd project

git switch -c feature-student

# Implement Student CRUD

git add .
git commit -m "Added Student CRUD API"

git push -u origin feature-student


Then create a Pull Request on GitHub.

Developer 2 commands:
git clone https://github.com/username/project.git

cd project

git switch -c feature-auth

# Implement authentication

git add .
git commit -m "Added JWT authentication"

git push -u origin feature-auth


Both developers create separate Pull Requests. After review and approval, merge them into main.

12. Top Git and GitHub Interview Questions
Question	Important concept
What is Git vs GitHub?	Local VCS vs cloud hosting
What is a repository?	Project tracked by Git
What is a commit?	Snapshot of changes
What is staging?	Preparing changes for commit
What is a branch?	Independent development line
What is merge?	Combining branches
What is rebase?	Reapplying commits on another base
Git pull vs fetch?	Fetch downloads; pull integrates
Merge vs rebase?	Merge preserves branch history; rebase rewrites commit history
What is a merge conflict?	Incompatible changes
What is a Pull Request?	Request to review and merge changes
What is fork?	Personal GitHub copy of a repository
What is .gitignore?	Specifies untracked files Git should ignore
What is HEAD?	Reference to the current checkout
What is cherry-pick?	Apply a specific commit elsewhere
What is revert vs reset?	Revert creates an undo commit; reset moves a branch reference
